                %path{:d => "M -2.484906 2.868153 C -2.031514 1.145472 -1.019261 0.334557 -0.00144804 0.000203555 C -1.020811 -0.333535 -2.034093 -1.147973 -2.485535 -2.867528 ", :fill => "none", :stroke => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "stroke-linecap" => "round", "stroke-linejoin" => "round", "stroke-miterlimit" => "10", "stroke-opacity" => "1", "stroke-width" => "0.47818", :transform => "matrix(-0.7879, 0.61575, 0.61575, 0.7879, 114.15889, 68.76245)"}
                %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                  %use{:x => "146.448", "xlink:href" => "#5JeeggB2tjICgSkh05qZitnXv2g=-glyph-1-3", :y => "56.709"}
                %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                  %use{:x => "151.533", "xlink:href" => "#5JeeggB2tjICgSkh05qZitnXv2g=-glyph-2-2", :y => "58.4665"}
            %p
              there is a unique homomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : \bigoplus_{i \in I} A_i \to K
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i = \phi \circ \iota_i
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in I
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              In these examples, the direct sum can also be described in more elementary terms as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
              of the direct product:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo :
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo :
                          %mi I
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi ess
                      %mo ∀
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo }
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\bigoplus_{i: I} A_i =
                    \\left\{
                    (a_i)_{i : I} \;|\; ess \forall (i: I),\; a_i = 0
                    \\right\}
                    \\,,
            %p
              where ‘
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ess
                    %mo ∀
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ess \forall
              ’ means ‘for all but finitely many’. This makes it clear that the direct sum equals the direct product when there are only finitely many objects involved.
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} =
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
              this is the group of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+linear+combinations"} formal linear combinations
              of elements in the summands.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
              or
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              are given by those on the underlying abelian groups.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              In the category of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+sets"} pointed sets
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge+sum"} wedge sum
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              pointed sets, this is different from the weak direct product (which is, as always, the same as the product for finitely many objects).
          .num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              In the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+spaces"} Banach spaces
              (with
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+linear+maps"} short linear maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi l
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l^1
              direct sum, while the weak direct product is the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi l
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l^\infty
              direct sum. (There is in fact a range of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi l
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l^p
              direct sums for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi p
                      %mo ≤
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leq p \leq \infty
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+properties"} universal properties
              they all satisfy.) In this case, the direct sum is the same as the coproduct, while the weak direct product is the same as the product even for infinitely many objects. See
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum+of+Banach+spaces"} direct sum of Banach spaces
          %h2#InternalDirectSum Internal direct sums
          %p
            Given an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/family"} family
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobjects"} subobjects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_i
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            (or more generally a family of morphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_i \to B
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_i A_i \to B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigoplus_i A_i
            exists. Suppose further that the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_i A_i \to B
            factors through the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_i A_i \to \bigoplus_i A_i
            (which means that it factors uniquely if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_i A_i \to \bigoplus_i A_i
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+map"} quotient map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigoplus_i A_i \to B
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+direct+sum"} internal direct sum
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_i
          %p
            In contrast, the abstractly defined direct sum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigoplus_i A_i
            may be called an
            = succeed "." do
              %strong external direct sum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+categories"} concrete categories
            where the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_i
            may either be given independently (for an external direct sum) or as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of some ambient space (either
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            or something of which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is a subset) for an internal direct sum. In too abstract a context, there is no difference: on the one hand, any internal direct sum is a fortiori isomorphic to any external direct sum; on the other hand, given an external direct sum, there is a natural map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_i A_i \to \bigoplus_i A_i
